        "minor": 0,
        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "The parser uses a child process and applies platform-specific limits, but its Unix working-directory setup does not satisfy the read-only requirement when the API runs as root on macOS.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api/Evidence/IsolatedPdfParser.cs",
          "line": 252,
          "message": "The Unix working-directory setup is writable by a root worker on macOS. Apply an effective read-only restriction or refuse to start the worker in this configuration.",
          "confidence": 0.82,
          "category": "security A01:2025",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "On macOS, Parse skips the Windows directory restriction and the Linux root-user isolation check. ChooseWorkingDirectory returns \"/\", and the child starts with the API process identity.",
            "failureScenario": "If the API runs as root on macOS, the worker can write to its working directory because root can write to \"/\". If a crafted PDF exploits the parser, the child can create files there despite AC-4 requiring a read-only working directory."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "agentId": "test-reviewer-codex",
      "reviewType": "testing",
      "harness": "codex",
      "harnessVersion": "0.162.0",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "approved": false,
      "findings": {
        "critical": 0,
        "major": 1,
        "minor": 0,
        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "The tests cover the listed parser and supervision behaviors, but the empty-environment test can pass when the child actually inherits the parent environment.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api.Tests/IsolatedPdfParserTests.cs",
          "line": 347,
          "message": "The empty-environment assertion does not detect removal of Environment.Clear(). Verify the environment received by a started child, or otherwise assert that CreateStartInfo explicitly configures an empty environment.",
          "confidence": 0.99,
          "category": "testing",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "The test calls CreateStartInfo and then Assert.Empty(startInfo.Environment). ProcessStartInfo.Environment is empty by default; reading it in the test initializes the collection.",
            "failureScenario": "If Environment.Clear() is removed from CreateStartInfo, the test still sees an empty collection and passes, while the launched child inherits the API process environment. AC-5 requires this test to fail when the empty environment is removed."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "agentId": "security-reviewer",
      "reviewType": "security",
      "harness": "codex",
      "harnessVersion": "0.162.0",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "approved": false,
      "findings": {
        "critical": 0,
        "major": 1,
        "minor": 0,
        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "The Windows working-directory ACL does not reliably remain read-only if the PDF worker is compromised. I found no other in-scope security vulnerability in the diff.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api/Evidence/IsolatedPdfParser.cs",
          "line": 198,
          "message": "The Windows worker can change the deny-write ACL on its own working directory because it runs as the directory owner. Use a restriction the child cannot change.",
          "confidence": 0.84,
          "category": "security A01:2025",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "On Windows, the API creates the worker directory and adds a deny-write ACL, then starts the child under the same identity that owns the directory. That identity can change the directory ACL.",
            "failureScenario": "On a Windows host, if a crafted PDF exploits the parser, code in the child can change the ACL and write files to its working directory. The directory therefore is not reliably read-only to the worker."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
